---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import TessExtrudeCanvas from '../../components/TessExtrudeCanvas.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout slug="tess-extrude">
  <TessExtrudeCanvas slot="scene" />

  <section>
    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p class="lead">Hand it a 2D outline, a few points or an SVG path, and get back a solid 3D shape ready for any Three.js scene.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      It runs a constrained Delaunay triangulation on the polygon's interior, using a WebAssembly port of the
      Triangle library. The result is a clean mesh of non-overlapping triangles that respects the boundary exactly,
      with no gaps and no slivers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cap is then mirrored, and the two faces are joined by side walls to form the final extruded volume.
      The scene above is a live example: a flat rectangle tessellated by the library, with each triangle painted on its own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Usage</h2>
    <pre><code>{`import { extrude } from 'tess-extrude';

const geometry = extrude(
  { type: 'points', points },
  { depth: 6, capDensity: 9, edgeSubdivisions: 3 },
);`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where you'd use it</h2>
    <ul class="list">
      <li>Turning SVG logos or icons into 3D objects for product configurators or hero scenes</li>
      <li>Extruded text in WebGL without font-specific geometry builders</li>
      <li>Floor plans or terrain patches that need real depth</li>
      <li>Any 2D outline that needs to become a solid, well-triangulated mesh</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
